# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
from fams import *
F = 'igappeal'
page('instagram-no-appeal-option-after-hack', F, 'Instagram Disabled After a Hack, No Appeal Option',
 'Your Instagram account was hacked and now shows disabled with no appeal button? Why the two problems overlap and which routes are still open.',
 'Instagram hacked and now disabled, with no appeal option?', 'No appeal option after a hack',
 'When a hacker gets the account disabled, the usual appeal screen can be missing. These are the routes that remain.',
 'If a hacker triggered the disabling, the account may show a message with no appeal button because the login details were changed. The appeal path is then the hacked-account recovery route, using a video selfie and the details Instagram already holds. Start with the notice and the email on file.',
 [('Treat it as a hack first', 'The recovery route for hacked accounts is different from the standard appeal.'),
  ('Check your email', 'Look for messages about changed email, phone or password. They show what was altered.'),
  ('Prepare the selfie', 'Hacked-account recovery often asks for a video selfie, so be ready for good light.')],
 [S('Why there is no button', 'The appeal button depends on the account state and the contact details attached to it. If a hacker changed the email or phone, the screen can show only an error or a login prompt.',
    ul(['The original email may have been replaced.', 'Two-factor authentication may now point at the hacker.', 'The disabled notice may only show on certain devices.'])),
  S('Routes to try', ol(['Search your inbox and spam for messages about a changed email or phone, and use the revert link if one is there.', 'Use Instagram’s own help flow for a hacked account and follow the video selfie steps.', 'Try from the device and network you used before the hack.', 'Write to us with the notice and the dates, and we will look at what fits.']))],
 [('Is this the same as an ordinary disabled account?', 'Not quite. A hacker’s involvement changes which route is available, so say so at the start.'),
  ('Should I keep trying the login?', 'Repeated attempts can trigger more locks. Try calmly, from your usual device.'),
  ('What if I never get an email?', 'Check spam, and which address is on file. If it was replaced, say that in your request.')],
 ['People who found a changed email and a disabled notice.', 'Business accounts that were hacked and shut down.', 'Anyone whose appeal button is missing after a takeover.'])

page('instagram-appeal-page-keeps-looping', F, 'Instagram Appeal Page Keeps Looping Back',
 'The Instagram appeal page sends you back to the start every time? Common causes for appeal loops and what to try instead of repeating the same steps.',
 'Instagram appeal page keeps sending you back?', 'Appeal page looping',
 'If the form loads and then resets, repeating it will not help. These are the usual causes and fixes.',
 'An appeal screen that resets usually means the form cannot confirm who you are, or the app or browser is stuck. Try another device and network, update or reinstall the app, and complete the form in one go. Screenshot each screen so the problem is clear if you need help.',
 [('Change the device', 'Try the browser if the app loops, or the app if the browser does.'),
  ('Change the network', 'Switch between Wi-Fi and mobile data. Avoid VPNs.'),
  ('Do it in one go', 'Starting the form and leaving it often resets the session.')],
 [S('Common reasons', ul(['An outdated app or cached data causing the form to reload.', 'A VPN, proxy or shared network.', 'The account state changed while the form was open.', 'An ID or selfie step that failed silently.']),
    'The loop is a symptom. The fix depends on where it resets.'),
  S('What to try', ol(['Update the app, or clear its cache, then log in again.', 'Open the appeal link from the notice email in a private browser window.', 'Complete every field and upload in one session, with a steady connection.', 'Screenshot the screen where it resets and note the time.']),
    'If it still loops, send us the screenshots and we will check which route fits.')],
 [('Is the loop a sign my appeal was rejected?', 'No. It is a technical problem, not a decision. Appeals that were sent show a confirmation.'),
  ('How do I know the appeal went through?', 'Look for a confirmation screen or an email. If there is neither, assume it did not.'),
  ('Can Unbanz fix the page?', 'No. We help you find another route and prepare the details.')],
 ['People who fill the form and end up back at the start.', 'Anyone whose submit button does nothing.', 'Users stuck on a verification step.'])

page('instagram-disabled-appeal-window-expired', F, 'Instagram Appeal Window Expired: What Now?',
 'Your Instagram disabled notice no longer shows an appeal option because the window ended? Other routes to check and how to approach them.',
 'Instagram appeal window expired?', 'Appeal window expired',
 'The usual appeal screen has closed. That does not always mean every route is closed.',
 'When the time to appeal ends, Instagram may say the account is permanently disabled and remove the button. Other routes can still exist, such as a newer notice, a recovery request tied to your identity, or a request through the help flow. Check the account on a different device first, then ask.',
 [('Check other devices', 'Notices can differ between the app and the web. Look at both.'),
  ('Look for a newer notice', 'A later email can open a different route than the first one.'),
  ('Be realistic', 'A closed window lowers the chances. A clear request is still worth a try.')],
 [S('What may still be open', ul(['A fresh notice in your email with a new link.', 'The help flow for a disabled account, from the login screen.', 'A request that goes through ID or selfie verification.', 'A business support route, if the account ran ads or a shop.']),
    'None of these is certain. They are the places to look before deciding it is over.'),
  S('Next steps', ol(['Log in on the web and in the app and screenshot what each shows.', 'Search email and spam for any message from Instagram about the account.', 'Submit what is offered, once, with a short and honest explanation.', 'If nothing is offered, contact us with the dates and screenshots.']))],
 [('Is permanently disabled really final?', 'Often it is, but there are situations where another route works. We tell you plainly if we see none.'),
  ('Should I make a new account meanwhile?', 'You can. Do not use it to contact the old account’s followers in bulk, which can lead to flags.'),
  ('Does Unbanz have a special route?', 'No. Unbanz has no special access. We use the same official routes available to you.')],
 ['People who missed the appeal window.', 'Owners of accounts that say permanently disabled.', 'Anyone who gave up early and wants a second look.'])

page('instagram-disabled-no-email-from-instagram', F, 'Instagram Account Disabled, No Email From Instagram',
 'Your Instagram account says disabled but you got no email? Why messages go missing, where to look and what to do when there is nothing to appeal.',
 'Instagram account disabled with no email?', 'Disabled with no email',
 'Not every disabled account comes with a message in your inbox. Here is where to look and what to do.',
 'Instagram does not always send an email when an account is disabled, and messages can go to an old address, to spam, or never arrive. Check every address you have used, the account’s notices on the login screen, and the in-app Account Status. If none shows anything, use the help flow for a disabled account.',
 [('Search every inbox', 'Old addresses and spam folders often hold the message.'),
  ('Check the login screen', 'The notice may appear only when you try to log in.'),
  ('Try the help flow', 'If there is no notice, the disabled-account help flow is the next route.')],
 [S('Why nothing arrives', ul(['The email on the account is old or was changed.', 'The message went to spam or a filter.', 'Instagram showed the notice in the app and sent no email.', 'A hacker replaced the address.']),
    'Searching for the word Instagram and the account name across all folders is the quickest check.'),
  S('What to do', ol(['Search all inboxes you have used for the username.', 'Open the app and the web login and screenshot any notice.', 'Use the help option from the login screen for a disabled account.', 'Send us the screenshots and the addresses you used, without passwords.']))],
 [('How do I know it is really disabled?', 'The login screen or app says so. If it only fails to log in, it may be a password or lock issue.'),
  ('Can I appeal without an email?', 'Sometimes, through the notice on the login screen or the help flow.'),
  ('What if the email on file is not mine?', 'It may have been changed by someone else. Treat it as a hacked account and tell us.')],
 ['People who see disabled on login but have no message.', 'Owners of accounts with outdated email addresses.', 'Hacked accounts where the email was replaced.'])

page('instagram-appeal-sent-twice-no-reply', F, 'Instagram Appeal Sent Twice, Still No Reply',
 'You appealed on Instagram twice and heard nothing? What silence usually means, why more submissions can hurt and what to check next.',
 'Instagram appeal sent twice with no reply?', 'Appeal sent twice, no reply',
 'Sending the same appeal again does not move it up the queue. Here is what helps instead.',
 'Silence after an appeal does not always mean a rejection. Requests can sit unanswered, or the reply may have gone to an old email. Stop sending duplicates, check every inbox and spam folder, and confirm the account state on the login screen. Then decide whether a different route fits.',
 [('Stop duplicates', 'More submissions do not speed up a decision and can add noise.'),
  ('Check where the reply went', 'Look at the address on the account and in spam.'),
  ('Look at the login screen', 'The account may already show a new state.')],
 [S('Why it goes quiet', ul(['The reply went to an address you do not check.', 'The appeal was incomplete, for example missing an ID or selfie step.', 'The request is still queued.', 'The account state changed, but no email was sent.']),
    'Instagram does not publish how long appeals take, and nobody outside can see the queue.'),
  S('What to do', ol(['Do not submit a third time until you know what happened to the first two.', 'Search all inboxes, including spam, for messages about the account.', 'Log in and screenshot the current notice and its date.', 'Send us the dates of both submissions and the screenshots.']))],
 [('How long should I wait?', 'There is no fixed time. Check the account and your email regularly rather than resubmitting.'),
  ('Is there someone at Meta to contact?', 'No direct line is open to the public, and we do not have one. We use the same official routes.'),
  ('What could a different route be?', 'Sometimes a request through ID or selfie verification, or the disabled-account help flow, fits better. We look at it with you.')],
 ['People who already submitted more than once.', 'Owners waiting a long time without any email.', 'Businesses that rely on the account.'])

page('instagram-appeal-says-permanently-disabled', F, 'Instagram Says Permanently Disabled: Options to Check',
 'Instagram told you your account is permanently disabled? What the wording means, what is still worth checking and when to stop spending effort.',
 'Instagram says your account is permanently disabled?', 'Permanently disabled message',
 'The wording sounds final, and often it is. These are the few things worth checking before you accept it.',
 'A permanently disabled message means Instagram decided the account broke its rules. Appeals can still work if the decision rested on a mistake, such as a wrongly flagged post or a hack, so check what the notice cites, then ask for a review of that exact point. If nothing is offered, there may be no route left.',
 [('Read the exact reason', 'The reason named in the notice shapes any appeal.'),
  ('Look for a mistake', 'A hack, a wrongly flagged post or a mixed-up account can justify a second look.'),
  ('Know when to stop', 'If no route is offered and the breach is clear, a new account may be the only option.')],
 [S('What may still help', ul(['Evidence that the account was hacked before it was disabled.', 'A clear explanation if the flagged content was misread.', 'Proof of identity when the account holder is in doubt.', 'A request through the disabled-account help flow, if the notice offers none.'])),
  S('How to approach it', ol(['Take a screenshot of the notice and write down the date.', 'Note what it cites, such as a post, behavior or policy.', 'Submit the review request once, addressing that point.', 'If nothing is offered, ask us whether any other route fits before moving on.']),
    'We will tell you directly if we think there is nothing to try. It saves you time.')],
 [('Can a permanently disabled account come back?', 'Sometimes, when the decision was wrong. It is Instagram’s decision, and no one can promise it.'),
  ('Is a new account a bad idea?', 'It is fine, as long as you do not repeat what caused the first one to be disabled.'),
  ('Does the wording differ by notice?', 'Yes. Some notices allow an appeal and some do not. Read yours carefully.')],
 ['People told their account is gone for good.', 'Businesses with a large following at stake.', 'Anyone wondering if another try is worth it.'])

page('instagram-appeal-code-never-arrives', F, 'Instagram Appeal Asks for a Code That Never Arrives',
 'The Instagram appeal or login asks for a security code that never comes? Reasons the code goes missing and routes that do not depend on it.',
 'Instagram security code for your appeal never arrives?', 'Appeal code never arrives',
 'No code means no way forward, so this is the one thing to fix. These are the likeliest reasons.',
 'A code that never arrives usually went to an email or number you can no longer reach, or was blocked by a filter or carrier. Check spam and the number on file, wait before asking again, and try the other option offered. If both are old, use the route for accounts where you lost your contact details.',
 [('Check the contact on file', 'The code goes to the address or number saved on the account, which may be old.'),
  ('Look in spam', 'Filters often catch security emails. Search by sender or by the word Instagram.'),
  ('Try the other option', 'Switch from email to phone or the reverse if the screen allows it.')],
 [S('Why codes go missing', ul(['A phone number that was changed or deactivated.', 'Email filtering or a full inbox.', 'Too many requests in a row, which can delay delivery.', 'A SIM or number that no longer belongs to you.'])),
  S('What to try', ol(['Request the code once and wait, instead of tapping repeatedly.', 'Check spam, promotions and other folders.', 'Switch between email and phone if both are on the account.', 'If neither works, use the help flow for lost access and tell us what you tried.']))],
 [('Can Unbanz receive the code for me?', 'No. Codes stay with you. We never ask for them.'),
  ('What if my phone number changed?', 'Use the route for people who can no longer reach their contact details, and be ready for a video selfie.'),
  ('Is the missing code a sign of a hack?', 'Not necessarily. If you also see unknown changes in email, it may be.')],
 ['People locked out because the code does not come.', 'Owners with an old email on the account.', 'Anyone who changed phone number.'])

page('instagram-appeal-approved-but-still-locked', F, 'Instagram Appeal Approved But Account Still Locked',
 'Instagram said your appeal worked but you still cannot get in? Common reasons, such as password resets, cached notices and pending verification.',
 'Instagram appeal approved but you cannot log in?', 'Approved but still locked',
 'An approval email and a locked screen can both be true. These are the usual causes.',
 'After an approved appeal, the account may still reject your login because the password is out of sync, an old session is cached, or a security step is pending. Reset the password through the official link, log in from your usual device and complete any code or selfie step before trying again.',
 [('Reset the password', 'Use the reset link in the approval email or the login screen.'),
  ('Clear the old session', 'Log out everywhere or reinstall the app so the old notice is gone.'),
  ('Finish pending steps', 'A code or selfie prompt may still need to be completed.')],
 [S('Why it can still fail', ul(['The app is showing a cached notice from before the approval.', 'The password was reset during the process and the new one has not been used.', 'A verification step is still waiting for you.', 'The approval email was a fake, sent by someone else.']),
    'Check the sender of the email. Real notices come from Instagram’s own addresses, and scammers copy them.'),
  S('What to do', ol(['Open the approval email and use the link inside it, after confirming the sender.', 'Reset the password and log in from your usual device.', 'Complete any security step the app shows.', 'If you are still locked after that, send us the screens you see.']))],
 [('Could the approval email be fake?', 'Yes. Check the sender, and do not enter your password on a site opened from a link you cannot verify.'),
  ('Do I need to appeal again?', 'Not unless a new notice appears. First complete the steps from the approval.'),
  ('What if the account is restored but empty?', 'Posts can be missing. Tell us what is gone and what you see.')],
 ['People who received an approval email.', 'Owners who see the same lock after approval.', 'Anyone unsure whether the email was real.'])

page('instagram-reinstated-then-disabled-again', F, 'Instagram Reinstated Then Disabled Again',
 'Your Instagram account came back and was disabled a second time? Why repeat actions happen and what to change before you appeal again.',
 'Instagram reinstated then disabled again?', 'Reinstated then disabled again',
 'A second disabling usually has a cause that was not removed. Find it before you appeal.',
 'When an account is disabled again soon after reinstatement, the cause usually continued: an automation tool, a hacker still holding a session, the same flagged content or shared logins. Change the password, log out all sessions, remove tools and posts that caused it, and then appeal once with those changes explained.',
 [('Find what continued', 'Look at tools, shared logins and flagged posts that stayed in place.'),
  ('Log out every session', 'A hacker or old device may still be active.'),
  ('Explain what changed', 'The second appeal should state what you removed.')],
 [S('Typical reasons', ul(['The same posts or listings that were flagged are still up.', 'A connected app or extension kept acting on the account.', 'A hacker kept a session open after the first recovery.', 'More than one person logs in from different places.']),
    'Repeat actions are handled more strictly, so the second appeal needs to show a real change.'),
  S('What to change first', ol(['Change the password and the email password, and enable two-factor authentication.', 'Log out of all devices from the security settings if you can reach them.', 'Remove or edit the content that was flagged.', 'Appeal from the notice and describe the changes.']))],
 [('Is a second disabling harder to reverse?', 'It can be. The appeal has to show what changed, not just ask again.'),
  ('Should I wait before appealing?', 'Fix the cause first. Appealing before that tends to repeat the outcome.'),
  ('Does Unbanz run the account?', 'No. We never log in. We help you review the cause and prepare the appeal.')],
 ['People who got the account back and lost it again.', 'Brands with several staff logins.', 'Owners who recovered from a hack.'])

page('instagram-disabled-no-reason-given', F, 'Instagram Account Disabled, No Reason Given',
 'Instagram disabled your account and the notice names no rule? How to find the likely cause from your own history and write an appeal anyway.',
 'Instagram disabled your account with no reason?', 'Disabled, no reason given',
 'Some notices say only that the account broke the rules. You can still work out the likely cause.',
 'A notice with no specific reason is common. Instead of guessing blindly, review what changed before it happened: new logins, tools, bulk activity, edited name or bio, flagged posts or reported comments. Then appeal once with a calm, specific explanation of how you use the account.',
 [('Build a timeline', 'List what changed in the days before the notice.'),
  ('Check Account Status', 'Settings may list removed posts or earlier warnings.'),
  ('Appeal with facts', 'Describe your normal use instead of arguing with the notice.')],
 [S('Where to look for a clue', ul(['Account Status for removed content or earlier warnings.', 'Email for messages about content removed from your posts.', 'Recent logins and connected apps in security settings.', 'Any recent change to the name, bio, link or profile photo.']),
    'The notice is generic on purpose. Your own history often shows the cause.'),
  S('Writing the appeal', ol(['State who you are and what the account is for.', 'Describe how you normally use it in two or three sentences.', 'Mention anything unusual you found, and what you have stopped.', 'Submit it once and keep the screenshot.']))],
 [('Will naming a reason in the appeal help?', 'Only if it is true. A wrong guess can hurt. Stick to what you know.'),
  ('Can Unbanz find the reason?', 'We cannot see inside Instagram. We help you read your own history and write the appeal.'),
  ('Is it likely a mistake?', 'It can be. It can also be an earlier flag you never saw. Check Account Status.')],
 ['People who got a generic disabled notice.', 'Creators and shops that rely on the account.', 'Owners who do not recall breaking any rule.'])
